### `spawn-policy-v3` / `spawn-policy-v4`

Each configured runtime declares an explicit admission set; model registry
rank/order does not grant admission.

`spawn-policy-v3` and `spawn-policy-v4` are two `contract_version` spellings
of the exact same wire shape: every key, input form, and validation rule
documented below applies identically to both. v4 adds no new field and
changes no admission behavior — it exists only as the version new
[v2 migration](#migrating-from-spawn-policy-v2) guidance now targets (decision
D8). A repository already on `spawn-policy-v3` is unaffected and undeprecated
this release; there is no requirement, automatic or otherwise, to move an
existing v3 config to v4.

The admission authority is the **admitted `(model, effort)` pair set**, not any
single config key. A runtime declares it with exactly one of two mutually
exclusive input forms:

- **`allowed_models`** — a flat model list under one shared effort bound. This is
  the degenerate case, adequate whenever every admitted model may run at the
  same efforts.
- **`entries`** — a list of combinable entries whose union is the pair set. Use
  it when different models need different effort bounds, which a flat list plus
  one shared bound provably cannot express: `equal(gpt-5.6-sol, low)` UNION
  `equal(gpt-5.3-codex-spark, xhigh)` has no `allowed_models` equivalent, since
  a shared bound of `xhigh` over-admits Sol and one of `low` under-admits Spark.

Both forms translate into the same pair set, and `project_config()` reports it
as `admitted_pairs`. Read that field rather than `allowed_models` when you need
to know what a policy actually admits: `allowed_models` is `null` under the
entries form, and even under its own form it cannot say which efforts survived
the bound.

| Runtime/role key | Type | Required | Allowed/default | Effect |
| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |
| `allowed_models` | non-empty array of unique model IDs | One admission form is required per runtime; optional role replacement | None | Flat input form; mutually exclusive with `entries`. |
| `entries` | non-empty array of entry objects | One admission form is required per runtime; optional role replacement | None | Combinable input form; the union of the entries is the admitted pair set. |
| `entries_expansion_digest` | `sha256:...` string | Required when an entry uses `less_or_equal`/`greater_or_equal` | None | Pins the ordered expansion; written only by `task-board spawn ceilings pin`. |
| `entries_expansion_pairs` | array of `model` or `model:effort` strings | Required beside the digest | None | Lets drift be reported as exact added/removed pairs. |
| `resolution_model` | admitted model ID string or `null` in a role | No | No fallback | Exact fallback when adjustment is allowed; must appear in the pair set. |
| `adjustment_confirmation` | string | Yes for runtime; optional role override | `required`, `none` | Whether adjusted resolution needs acknowledgement. |
| `reasoning_effort` | string | No | Runtime default | Shared effort bound; not accepted beside `entries`. |
| `reasoning_effort_criterion` | string | Required with a directional effort bound | `less_or_equal`, `greater_or_equal` | Effort direction; not accepted beside `entries`. |
| `roles.<role>.*` | same fields | No | Inherit runtime field-by-field | Role replacement/override. |
| `roles.<role>.default_model` | model ID string | No | No default | Role selection default; see [Per-role model preferences](#per-role-model-preferences). |
| `roles.<role>.default_reasoning_effort` | effort string | No | No default | Role selection default; Codex/Qwen/Claude only. |

#### Ceiling entries

Each entry is `{criterion, model, reasoning_effort?}`. The union is a plain OR:
entry order is not significant, and **no entry can subtract from another**.

| Entry key | Type | Required | Allowed | Effect |
| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |
| `criterion` | string | Yes | `equal`, `less_or_equal`, `greater_or_equal` | Governs **both** dimensions of the entry. |
| `model` | runtime-owned model ID | Yes | — | The bound both dimensions are read against. |
| `reasoning_effort` | string | No | Runtime effort vocabulary | Effort bound; omitted bounds no effort. Rejected for Gemini, Muse, and Agy (Antigravity encodes effort in the model ID). |

The criterion governs the effort dimension too, so an entry reads as
`criterion(model, reasoning_effort)`:

- `equal(gpt-5.3-codex-spark, xhigh)` admits Spark **at `xhigh` and nowhere
  else**. A Spark request below it is refused, not lifted.
- `less_or_equal(gpt-5.6-luna, high)` admits every model at or below Luna by
  capability rank, at every effort at or below `high`.
- `greater_or_equal(gpt-5.6-terra, high)` is the mirror: at or above, in both
  dimensions.

One criterion for both dimensions is what makes the pair set exact. Under a
hardcoded effort ceiling no input form could ever admit a model at a single
effort, and `equal(sol, low)` UNION `equal(spark, xhigh)` would silently admit
`spark/low`.

A role that configures `entries` replaces the runtime's admission form
wholesale — the inherited `allowed_models` **and** the inherited runtime effort
bound are dropped, because each entry already carries its own bound. Omitting
both forms in a role inherits the runtime policy unchanged.

```json
{
  "spawn": {
    "ceilings": {
      "contract_version": "spawn-policy-v3",
      "codex": {
        "entries": [
          {"criterion": "equal", "model": "gpt-5.6-sol", "reasoning_effort": "low"},
          {"criterion": "equal", "model": "gpt-5.3-codex-spark", "reasoning_effort": "xhigh"}
        ],
        "resolution_model": "gpt-5.6-sol",
        "adjustment_confirmation": "none"
      }
    }
  }
}
```

#### Pinning an ordered expansion

`less_or_equal` and `greater_or_equal` resolve model membership by comparing
runtime-local capability rank. Capability rank is evidence, not an admission
authority, so an ordered expansion may only be used once an operator has
approved the exact pair set it produces:

```bash
task-board spawn ceilings pin              # every configured runtime and role
task-board spawn ceilings pin --agent codex --role reviewer
```

This command is the only writer of `entries_expansion_digest`; nothing writes it
implicitly. Until it runs, an ordered entry set fails closed with
`spawn_ceiling_admission_drift` naming the config key and the next step. If a
later, truthful re-rank moves the expansion, the same failure lists the exact
added and removed pairs — admission never widens or narrows silently. `--agent`
or `--role` matching no configured entry target is an error naming the selector,
not a successful no-op.

`equal`-only entry sets never consult capability rank, cannot drift, and are
never pinned; configuring a digest for one is rejected.

### Migrating from `spawn-policy-v2`

Nothing rewrites `task-board.config.json` automatically — a `spawn-policy-v2`
repository stays byte-identical until an operator edits it by hand. `task-board
q 'project_config()'` still reports the migration evidence needed to do that
edit, under `spawn.ceilings`:

| Field | Meaning |
| --- | --- |
| `migration_required` | `true` whenever any v2 runtime is configured. |
| `migration_target` | `"spawn-policy-v4"` — the version a v2 repository is now steered to, per decision D8. `spawn-policy-v3` remains a fully supported target too; it is simply no longer the one this field names. |
| `<runtime>.migration` | The exact `spawn-policy-v4`-shaped runtime object — `allowed_models`, `resolution_model`, `adjustment_confirmation`, and (when configured) `reasoning_effort`/`reasoning_effort_criterion` — that preserves the effective v2 ceiling. Paste it under `spawn.ceilings.<runtime>` (or `.roles.<role>`) once `contract_version` is set to `spawn-policy-v4`; no field needs editing first. |
| `<runtime>.migration_warning.reason` | `spawn_policy_v2_explicit_migration_required` (a `model`/`model_criterion` bound is configured) or `spawn_policy_v2_effort_only_migration_required` (only `reasoning_effort` is). Frozen: a caller may key on either string across releases. |
| `<runtime>.migration_warning.next_step` | Human-readable instructions. Not frozen — only the `reason` codes above are a stable contract. |

The projected object is computed through the same frozen
`spawn-policy-v2-rollout-*` compatibility snapshot the live v2 resolver
enforces (never live capability rank), so pasting it produces the identical
admitted `(model, effort)` pair set the v2 ceiling already granted — verified
        "openai": ["3m", "10m", "30m", "90m"],
        "alibaba": ["2m", "8m", "20m"]
      }
