Validate board structure

Usage:
  task-board validate [flags]

Flags:
  -h, --help   help for validate

Global Flags:
      --board-dir string         Path to the board directory (overrides TASK_BOARD_DIR and config local.board_dir) (default ".task-board")
      --context-profile string   Agent context profile override: full or lite
      --insecure                 Skip TLS certificate verification (for self-signed certs)
      --json                     Output in JSON format
      --no-update-check          Disable automatic update check
      --remote string            Remote board server URL (overrides TASK_BOARD_REMOTE)
      --remote-board string      Remote board ID (overrides TASK_BOARD_ID)
Queue an AI agent run (agy, claude, codex, gemini, muse, or qwen) for a board element.

spawn.preferred_agentic_system is an optional provider allow-set. exclusive
pins one provider; mixed retains runtime affinity when it belongs to the set or
accepts an explicit in-set --agent. Out-of-set providers are refused before
task lookup or launch side effects. Legacy spawn.preferred_agent retains its
best-effort preference behavior. Runtime affinity detects Codex from
CODEX_SESSION, CODEX_THREAD_ID, or CODEX_CI and otherwise selects Claude.
Optional spawn.schedule UTC windows temporarily replace the base provider set.
A provider or registered model outside the active window is refused before
side effects with the allowed set and a concrete retry step; the selected
provider's ceiling is applied afterward.

On launch: assigns element to agent name, sets status from role config and
queues a tracked background RUN. By default the public command returns
immediately with observation commands. Pass --wait to block in the same process
until the RUN reaches a terminal state; no separate 'task-board spawn wait
RUN-...' invocation is needed. The --background flag is required so execution placement is explicit
at every callsite.

Prompt composition:
  Primary source of truth is the board:
    - Task description comes from the element README
    - Task-specific DoD comes from the element checklist
    - Instructions come from precondition resources attached to the element

  Legacy compatibility overrides:
    --task-path FILE  Optional extra brief appended to the task description
    --dod-path FILE   Optional extra DoD lines appended to the prompt only

The prompt is always composed from the role template plus board state. Legacy
file overrides only append extra context — they never replace role identity,
board task context, FIRST/LAST status blocks, skills, or CLI reference.

Safety:
  --instruction NAME  Validates that a precondition resource matching NAME
                      is linked to the task before spawning. Errors with a
                      helpful message if not found.

Prompt file is auto-saved to .temp/prompts/ with timestamp naming.

Model and reasoning selection:
  --model is required for every spawn of a role without a configured default.
  --reasoning-effort is required for Codex spawns.
  --reasoning-effort is required for Qwen spawns.
  --reasoning-effort is required for Claude spawns, except claude-haiku-4-5
  (and its dated snapshot), which take no effort at all.
  task-board never supplies model or reasoning defaults of its own. The only
  exception is a per-role default the project configured explicitly at
  spawn.ceilings.<provider>.roles.<role>.default_model and
  .default_reasoning_effort. That seed fills only the dimensions omitted at the
  callsite, an explicit flag always wins per dimension, and the seeded pair
  still passes the ordinary ceiling, so a default can never widen a bound. A
  role with no configured default keeps explicit selection mandatory. Inspect
  what a role resolves to before spawning with:
    task-board q 'project_config(view=spawn-preflight, role=ROLE, agent=PROVIDER)'
  Inspect the current registry with: task-board q 'models(agent=codex)'

  With model_criterion less_or_equal or greater_or_equal, every spawn also
  requires --selection-rationale with one non-empty line explaining the chosen
  pair. If policy changes the requested pair, spawn records the requested-pair
  rationale tuple in task notes, creates no run, and reports requested ->
  resolved. Re-evaluate the resolved pair, then either re-request within
  bounds/escalate or re-invoke with --ack-resolved <model>/<effort> matching it
  exactly and fresh rationale text. After whitespace normalization that text
  must differ byte-for-byte from every rationale already recorded for the task
  and role. Pass-through ordered selections do not use --ack-resolved.
  model_criterion equal uses neither flag and forbids selection assessments.

  spawn-policy-v3 uses the admitted (model, effort) pair set as the sole
  admission authority; allowed_models and entries are two mutually exclusive
  input forms that both translate into it, and neither is itself the
  authority. spawn-policy-v4 is the identical wire shape under a new
  contract_version spelling -- the version v2 migration guidance now targets --
  and everything below applies to it unchanged.
  PolicyRank, registry order, lifecycle, and recommendation never change that
  set; an ordered entry consults capability rank only against an
  operator-approved entries_expansion_digest. An out-of-set model is refused, or resolved to the exact configured
  resolution_model. adjustment_confirmation=required uses the rationale/ack
  boundary above; none forbids both flags. Inspect the target-effective policy
  with project_config(view=spawn-preflight, role=ROLE, agent=PROVIDER).
  Grandfathered v2 ordered and effort-only policies use the frozen compatibility
  snapshot. Their project_config migration objects are read-only; task-board
  never rewrites repository config automatically.

  Optimize maximum task quality per token: assess scope, complexity, risk,
  required autonomy, validation burden, and expected context and token cost before launch.
  For board-managed Codex work, max is the highest recommended effort. ultra
  remains compatible on supported models but adds model-internal delegation;
  prefer task-board roles, RUN lifecycle, dependencies, and reviewer routing
  for predictable delegation.

Workload-aware recommendations:
  When spawn.workload_classes is configured, first query the target
  spawn-preflight with task_class=... or workload_class=.... Reason from the
  returned available pairs, ranking, provenance, dropped reasons, and snapshot.
  Then explicitly spawn with exactly one of --workload-task-class or
  --workload-class, plus the exact --recommendation-snapshot-digest and one
  non-empty --recommendation-rationale line. The selected available pair may
  follow or consciously reject the ranking. A recommendation never supplies a
  missing model/effort, widens authority, or auto-selects a pair.

Examples:
  # Queue a Codex run with an explicit model and reasoning choice
  task-board spawn TASK-260214-abc123 --role developer --background --agent codex --model gpt-5.6-sol --reasoning-effort max

  # Workload-aware explicit selection after a matching spawn-preflight query
  task-board spawn TASK-260214-abc123 --role developer --background --agent codex --model gpt-5.6-luna --reasoning-effort high --workload-task-class code --recommendation-snapshot-digest sha256:... --recommendation-rationale "Bounded implementation; Luna retains every validation gate"

  # Validate instruction resource is linked before spawning
  task-board spawn TASK-260214-abc123 --role developer --background --agent codex --model gpt-5.6-sol --reasoning-effort max --instruction impl-guide.md

  # Spawn with specific model and budget
  task-board spawn TASK-260214-abc123 --role developer --background --agent claude --model claude-opus-5 --budget 5.0 \
    --instruction impl-guide.md

  # Queue a balanced Codex model with an explicit effort
  task-board spawn TASK-260214-abc123 --role developer --background --agent codex --model gpt-5.6-terra --reasoning-effort max

  # Queue a Qwen Token Plan model with an explicit effort
  task-board spawn TASK-260214-abc123 --role developer --background --agent qwen --model qwen3.7-plus --reasoning-effort xhigh

  # Inspect and wait on tracked runs later
  task-board spawn status RUN-260320-abc123
  task-board spawn wait RUN-260320-abc123

  # Queue and wait for one run in the same CLI process
  task-board spawn TASK-260214-abc123 --role developer --background --agent codex --model gpt-5.6-sol --reasoning-effort max --wait

  # Replay and follow durable run events
  task-board spawn events RUN-260320-abc123
  task-board spawn watch RUN-260320-abc123

  # Inspect and request durable run directives
  task-board spawn directives RUN-260320-abc123
  task-board spawn directive RUN-260320-abc123 cancel

Watcher notifications are external observation surfaces by default. When the
orchestrator session is launched through task-board codex, tracked terminal
updates are also injected back into the active Codex thread via the runtime
adapter. Inside a Claude Code session (CLAUDECODE env set) there is no
injection API; instead, run the printed wait command as a harness background
task — the harness wakes the session when the child reaches terminal state, so
the orchestrator can fan out several children and keep working while one
background wait per RUN reports back.

For the fused --wait path, the caller still decides harness placement. A Claude
orchestrator can wrap the single 'task-board spawn ... --wait' invocation in
run_in_background; task-board does not emulate or move that harness decision.

Spawn response contract:
  Spawn returns coordinator-facing observation hints for the tracked run:
    - cursor token (RUN-ID:seq)
    - spawn events replay command
    - spawn watch follow command
    - spawn wait barrier command
  JSON output exposes these as observation.*.
  observation.runtime is codex-session, claude-session, or external;
  observation.delivery names the matching non-blocking completion channel
  (codex-in-session, claude-background-wait, or external-watch).
  observation.inSessionDelivery is true only when a runtime-specific adapter
  is active for the current Codex session.

Usage:
  task-board spawn <TASK-ID> [flags]
  task-board spawn [command]

Available Commands:
  ceilings    Inspect and maintain spawn ceiling admission policy
  directive   Request a durable control directive for a spawn run
  directives  List durable directives recorded for a spawn run
  events      Read durable spawn run events with optional follow mode
  goal        Show the active immutable board goal for a tracked run
  observe     Wait for terminal state and return event delta plus handoff evidence
  status      Show spawn run status
  wait        Wait for a spawn run to finish
  watch       Follow durable spawn run events and stream watcher notifications

Flags:
      --ack-resolved string                     Exact resolved model/effort pair acknowledged after an ordered-criterion adjustment
      --agent string                            Runtime ID to target: a configured runtime (built-in: agy, claude, codex, gemini, muse, qwen; plus any spawn.runtimes declaration) (overrides affinity within preferred_agentic_system; legacy preferred_agent remains best-effort)
      --background                              Launch in background and return a tracked RUN handle
      --budget float                            Deprecated as a control: max spend in USD (Claude prompt mode). Never defaulted from config; prompt quality is the control, not a spend cap
      --context stringArray                     Context to inherit; repeatable, first is primary and later values are supplemental (default: deterministic auto selection)
      --goal-scope string                       Comma-separated board scope for a goal-bound orchestrator pool
  -h, --help                                    help for spawn
      --instruction string                      Precondition resource name to validate before spawning (contains match)
      --model string                            Model ID, required unless the role has a configured default_model (e.g. claude-opus-4-8, gpt-5.6-sol, qwen3.7-plus)
      --no-context                              Suppress context lookup entirely; mutually exclusive with --context
      --reasoning-effort string                 Required for Codex/Qwen/Claude unless the role has a configured default_reasoning_effort: low, medium, high, xhigh (Extra high), max; Codex also supports ultra on selected models; Claude Haiku takes no effort at all
      --recommendation-rationale string         Required one-line reason for following or consciously rejecting the workload recommendation
      --recommendation-snapshot-digest string   Fresh content-bound digest returned by workload-aware spawn-preflight
      --register-context string                 Publish this run's writable context child on success: project, role, or task (project requires the context-builder role)
      --role string                             Agent role name (loads .roles/<name>.md with identity and skill refs)
      --selection-rationale string              Required one-line justification when the effective model criterion is less_or_equal or greater_or_equal
      --timeout string                          Timeout duration (e.g. 30m, 1h)
      --wait                                    Block until the tracked RUN reaches a terminal state (no separate spawn wait needed)
      --workload-class string                   Explicit workload class from spawn-preflight (configured recommendation policy only)
      --workload-task-class string              Task class used by spawn-preflight to derive the workload class (configured recommendation policy only)

Global Flags:
      --board-dir string         Path to the board directory (overrides TASK_BOARD_DIR and config local.board_dir) (default ".task-board")
      --context-profile string   Agent context profile override: full or lite
      --insecure                 Skip TLS certificate verification (for self-signed certs)
      --json                     Output in JSON format
      --no-update-check          Disable automatic update check
      --remote string            Remote board server URL (overrides TASK_BOARD_REMOTE)
      --remote-board string      Remote board ID (overrides TASK_BOARD_ID)

Use "task-board spawn [command] --help" for more information about a command.
